page("how-to-light-charcoal-without-lighter-fluid","How to Light Charcoal Without Lighter Fluid","Chimney starter, paper, natural fire starters and wood kindling: four clean ways to light charcoal in the Slot Grill Compact, plus wind, wet-weather and timing tips.",IMG["c28"],f"""
<p>Lighter fluid is the most common way people ruin a good cook on a small charcoal grill. Pour it on, and in the first minutes you get a tall flare that wind loves to push sideways. Pour too soon before cooking, and food can pick up a petroleum taste. On a compact grill where the food sits close to the coals, both problems get worse. The good news: you don't need it. Here are four reliable ways to light the Slot Grill Compact cleanly, with timing, wind and wet-weather tips.</p>
<h2>Method 1: Chimney starter (the best all-around way)</h2><p>A chimney starter is a metal tube with a grate near the bottom and a handle. Coals go in the top, a fire source goes underneath, and the chimney shape draws air up through the charcoal so it lights fast and evenly.</p><ol><li>Set the chimney on a non-combustible surface, or right inside the Compact's firebox on the coal bed.</li><li>Crumple two or three sheets of newspaper loosely (or use one or two natural fire starters) and put them under the chimney grate. Don't pack paper tight; it needs air.</li><li>Fill the chimney with charcoal. For a quick cook for one or two on the Compact, a half chimney is usually enough.</li><li>Light the paper or starter in two or three spots.</li><li>Leave it alone. Flames will appear at the top in several minutes.</li><li>Pour when the top coals are lightly covered in gray ash. Briquettes usually take longer than lump to get there.</li><li>Wear heat-safe gloves, pour slowly toward one end of the firebox to build a <a href="/two-zone-fire-on-a-small-grill.html">two-zone fire</a>, and set the empty chimney on a safe surface to cool.</li></ol><p class="note">Tip: a drizzle of cooking oil on the newspaper makes it burn longer, which helps on cold or damp days.</p>
<h2>Method 2: Natural fire starters in the firebox</h2><p>Wax-and-wood or wood-wool fire starter cubes work without a chimney. Put one or two in the Compact's firebox, build a small pyramid of charcoal over them, light, and wait until the coals ash over before spreading them. This takes longer than a chimney and the pile needs to stay tight to catch, but it's light to pack for trips.</p>
<h2>Method 3: Paper and kindling under a charcoal pile</h2><ol><li>Crumple a few sheets of newspaper into loose balls in the firebox.</li><li>Lay a small teepee or crisscross of dry kindling (pencil to thumb thickness) over the paper.</li><li>Pile charcoal on the kindling.</li><li>Light the paper. Once the kindling is going well, gently add a few more coals.</li><li>Wait for the coals to ash over, then spread into zones.</li></ol><p>This is the campfire method and it's handy if you forgot the chimney. Use only untreated, dry kindling.</p>
<h2>Method 4: Start with wood</h2><p>The Compact burns cooking wood as well as charcoal. Build a small wood fire, let it burn down to a bed of glowing coals, and either cook over those or pour charcoal on top of the burning wood to catch. This gives the most flavor and is a good choice at <a href="/hunting-camp-cooking-compact-grill.html">camp</a> where there's dry deadfall you're allowed to use.</p>
<h2>How long does it take?</h2><p>It depends on fuel, wind and temperature, so watch the coals rather than the clock. Ready coals glow red under a thin coat of gray ash. If they're still black on top, wait. Cooking over coals that aren't ready gives weak heat, smoke and sticking.</p>
<h2>Lighting in wind</h2><ul><li>Set up so a vehicle, wall or rock outcrop blocks the wind, keeping safe clearance from anything that burns.</li><li>Light the downwind side of the paper so the flame is pushed into the fuel, not away from it.</li><li>Expect wind to make coals burn faster and hotter on the windward side. Account for that when you build zones.</li></ul><blockquote>"I lit up the charcoal in minutes in windy conditions."<cite>Richard Shapero, Compact owner</cite></blockquote>
<h2>Lighting in cold or damp weather</h2><ul><li>Keep charcoal in a dry bag or lidded bin. Damp charcoal is the number one cause of "it won't light."</li><li>Use more paper or two starters, and oil the paper lightly.</li><li>Light more fuel than you would on a warm day; cold steel and cold air pull heat away.</li></ul>
<h2>Why not lighter fluid?</h2><ul><li>It flares in wind and can carry flame further than you expect.</li><li>If food goes on before it has burned off completely, it can taste.</li><li>Never add lighter fluid to coals that are already lit, and never use gasoline or other accelerants.</li></ul>
<h2>Troubleshooting</h2><div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Fix</th></tr><tr><td>Paper burns out and coals don't catch</td><td>Paper packed too tight or too little. Use loose balls, add a starter cube, oil the paper.</td></tr><tr><td>Only the bottom coals lit</td><td>Give it more time; the chimney works bottom-up.</td></tr><tr><td>Coals lit but die after pouring</td><td>Poured too early, ash buildup choking the bed, or coals spread too thin. Wait for ash over, empty old ash, keep coals together.</td></tr><tr><td>Lots of smoke and a sharp smell</td><td>Coals not fully lit. Wait.</td></tr></table></div>
<h2>What to pack for lighting</h2><ul><li>Chimney starter (or natural starter cubes)</li><li>Newspaper in a zip bag</li><li>Two lighters or a lighter and waterproof matches</li><li>Heat-safe gloves</li><li>Dry charcoal</li></ul>
{QUICK}
{SAFETY}
""","fire",[("Can I light charcoal without lighter fluid?","Yes. A chimney starter with newspaper or natural fire starters is the cleanest and most reliable way. Paper and kindling, or starting with a small wood fire, also work."),("How do I know charcoal is ready to cook on?","The coals glow red under a light coat of gray ash. Black coals on top mean they need more time."),("Can I put the chimney starter inside the Slot Grill Compact?","You can set it on the coal bed in the firebox, which keeps the hot chimney over the grill instead of on a table. Make sure it's stable and wear heat-safe gloves."),("Why won't my charcoal light?","The most common causes are damp charcoal, paper packed too tightly, not enough paper, or pouring the coals before they were fully lit.")])

page("charcoal-temperature-control-small-grill","Temperature Control on a Small Open Charcoal Grill","How to run hot, medium and low heat on the Slot Grill Compact without a lid or vents: coal amount, coal placement, the hand test, wind, and adding fuel.",IMG["c08"],f"""
<p>The Slot Grill Compact is an open charcoal grill: no lid, no dampers, no dials. That sounds like less control, but it's actually simpler. You control heat with three things you can see: <b>how much</b> charcoal is lit, <b>where</b> you put it, and <b>where</b> you put the food. Learn those and you can run anything from a hard steak sear to gentle eggs on the griddle.</p>
<h2>The three levers</h2><div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Lever</th><th>More heat</th><th>Less heat</th></tr><tr><td>Amount of lit fuel</td><td>More coals, piled deeper</td><td>Fewer coals, single layer</td></tr><tr><td>Coal placement</td><td>Pile under the food</td><td>Move coals away; leave an empty zone</td></tr><tr><td>Food placement</td><td>Over the deep coals</td><td>Over the thin or empty zone</td></tr></table></div><p>On the Compact the food sits close to the coals, so these levers act fast. Moving a steak four inches toward the cool end changes its heat more than you'd expect.</p>
<h2>Reading the heat: the hand test</h2><p>Hold your palm flat about where the food will sit, over the zone you want to check, and count how long you can comfortably keep it there.</p><div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Seconds</th><th>Heat level</th><th>Good for</th></tr><tr><td>About 2</td><td>High</td><td>Searing steaks, smash burgers, skirt steak, blistering peppers</td></tr><tr><td>4–5</td><td>Medium</td><td>Burgers, sausages, pork chops, shrimp, most vegetables</td></tr><tr><td>7+</td><td>Low</td><td>Finishing chicken, holding food, warming tortillas, eggs on the griddle</td></tr></table></div><p>It's approximate. Use it to pick a zone, and use an <a href="{THERMO}">instant-read thermometer</a> to decide when food is done.</p>
<h2>Setting heat for common cooks</h2><ul><li><b>High and fast</b> (steak, skirt, smash burgers): a fuller chimney, coals piled under most of the grate, a small cool zone for rescue.</li><li><b>Medium two-zone</b> (burgers, chops, sausages, wings): coals across two-thirds, last third empty.</li><li><b>Low and gentle</b> (bone-in chicken start, fish on the griddle, eggs): a single layer of coals, or food kept on the cool end.</li><li><b>Griddle</b>: the griddle plate spreads heat. Put more coals under the area you want hot and fewer where you want to hold food.</li></ul>
<h2>Turning heat up mid-cook</h2><ol><li>Push coals together with tongs. Spread-out coals lose heat quickly.</li><li>Knock ash off the coals by tapping the side of a coal with tongs; ash insulates.</li><li>Add a handful of fresh charcoal to the hot side. Lump catches faster than briquettes.</li><li>For big boosts, pour in lit coals from a second chimney instead of cold ones.</li></ol>
<h2>Turning heat down mid-cook</h2><ol><li>Move food to the cool side first. It's instant.</li><li>Spread coals out or push some toward the far end.</li><li>Lift the food off the heat for a minute onto a clean tray.</li><li>Don't spray water on coals to cool them; it throws ash and steam onto the food.</li></ol>
<h2>Weather changes everything</h2><ul><li><b>Wind</b> feeds the coals and makes them burn hotter and faster on the windward side. Point the hot end into the wind, or shelter the grill with safe clearance.</li><li><b>Cold</b> pulls heat from the steel and the food. Start with more fuel.</li><li><b>Rain or damp</b> slows lighting. Keep fuel dry and allow extra time.</li></ul>
<h2>Keeping a steady fire for longer cooks</h2><p>For anything over about 30–40 minutes, plan to feed the fire. Add a few coals to the hot side every 20–30 minutes so new fuel catches before the old fuel fades. Clear ash from the previous cook before you light; ash in the bottom of the Compact's firebox restricts the airflow that keeps coals burning. Between cooks, once ash is cold, you can pull one side panel, empty it, and slide the panel back in.</p>
<h2>Why the Compact's design helps</h2><ul><li><b>Thick steel</b> (12-gauge carbon steel) holds and evens out heat better than thin sheet metal.</li><li><b>Air gap</b>: 2⅞" between the table and the bottom of the coal bed helps minimize radiant heat to the surface below.</li><li><b>Two-piece top</b>: run a grate and a <a href="{GRID}">griddle</a> side by side and use each over a different heat zone.</li></ul>
<h2>Troubleshooting</h2><div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Fix</th></tr><tr><td>Everything cooks too fast</td><td>Fewer coals, bigger cool zone, more time on the cool side</td></tr><tr><td>Can't get a sear</td><td>More coals, piled deeper; preheat grates; pat food dry</td></tr><tr><td>Hot and cold spots on the griddle</td><td>Spread coals more evenly under it, or use the spots on purpose</td></tr><tr><td>Fire fades after 30 minutes</td><td>Add coals earlier, clear ash, consolidate coals</td></tr></table></div>
{TEMPS}
{QUICK}
{MISTAKES}
""","fire",[("How do you control temperature on a grill with no lid or vents?","By the amount of lit charcoal, where you place the coals, and where you place the food. A two-zone fire gives you a hot side and a cool side to move food between."),("What is the hand test for grill heat?","Hold your palm where the food will cook. About 2 seconds before you need to pull away is high heat, 4 to 5 seconds is medium, and 7 or more is low. It is approximate; use a thermometer for doneness."),("How do I make the fire hotter quickly?","Push the coals together, knock off the ash, and add lit coals from a second chimney or a handful of lump to the hot side."),("Should I spray water on coals to cool them down?","No. Move the food to the cool side instead. Water kicks ash and steam onto the food.")])
